<p class="lead">A few simple rules keep the library useful and safe for everyone.</p>

<h2>Welcome</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Lecture slides and handouts your teacher shared with the class</li>
  <li>Your own notes, summaries, formula sheets and solved examples</li>
  <li>Assignments, lab manuals and lab reports you wrote yourself</li>
  <li>Previous midterm, final and quiz questions from semesters that have finished</li>
  <li>Free or openly licensed books and references</li>
</ul>

<h2>Please do not upload</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Full commercial textbooks</strong> or any file you do not have the right to share</li>
  <li><strong>Questions from an exam that is still running</strong> or has not been held yet</li>
  <li>Another student's assignment or project passed off as an example to copy</li>
  <li>Files with personal details such as phone numbers, ID cards or private chat screenshots</li>
  <li>Anything harmful, offensive or unrelated to studying, including files that contain malware</li>
</ul>

<h2>Make your upload easy to find</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Choose the right department and course. If the course is missing, add it with its official code.</li>
  <li>Write a clear title, such as "Chapter 3 Linked Lists (slides)" or "Final 2025 Spring".</li>
  <li>For previous questions, pick the exam type and add the semester.</li>
  <li>Use the description for the teacher's name and the topics covered.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What happens after you upload</h2>
<p>A moderator checks your file, usually within a few days. If it is approved, it appears on the course page. If it is rejected, you will see the reason on your <a href="/my">My uploads</a> page, and you are welcome to fix the problem and upload again.</p>

<h2>Reporting a problem</h2>
<p>Open any file and use "Report a problem" if it is in the wrong course, is spam, is copyrighted or is inappropriate. If you are the rights holder, see <a href="/copyright">Copyright and takedown</a>.</p>
